Q: How to test Vue ripple directive has been used? A: <p>Your mock is not being called because <code>v-ripple</code> has already been registered by the Quasar plugin in your global test setup:</p>
<p><code>config.global.plugins = [[Quasar, { plugins: { LocalStorage } }]]</code></p>
<p>By the time you call <code>mount()</code>, Vue is already using <em>Quasar's</em> directive, so the one you pass in <code>global.directives</code> is never used.</p>
<p>If you just want to make sure that your component applies the directive do not install Quasar for that test. Instead, register a stub directive and spy on its lifecycle hook:</p>
<pre class="lang-js prettyprint-override"><code>import { mount } from '@vue/test-utils'
import { vi, expect } from 'vitest'

const beforeMount = vi.fn()

mount(MyComponent, {
  global: {
    directives: {
      ripple: {
        beforeMount,
      },
    },
  },
})

expect(beforeMount).toHaveBeenCalled()
</code></pre>
<p>Also you registered the directive as <code>Ripple</code>, and it should be <code>ripple</code>.</p>
<p>If Quasar is installed globally, this approach won't work because Quasar's own <code>v-ripple</code> registration takes precedence. In that case you'd need to either avoid installing Quasar for this specific test or take a different testing approach (e.g: assert the directive exists).</p>

Q: Fastest way to read millions of integers from stdin C++? A: <p>For reading the input if you have mmap you can use something like this to map the file</p>
<pre><code>#ifdef MAP_POPULATE
    constexpr int kFileFlags = MAP_PRIVATE | MAP_POPULATE;
#else
    constexpr int kFileFlags = MAP_PRIVATE;
#endif
    char* start = (char*)mmap(nullptr, (size_t)filelen, PROT_READ, kFileFlags, STDIN_FILENO, 0);

</code></pre>
<p>Then you can hint that you are about to use some part with the following line to prefetch the data into the cache:</p>
<pre><code>_mm_prefetch(start + offset, _MM_HINT_T0);
</code></pre>
<p>Prefetching has to be done ahead of your use to be efficient and in blocks.</p>
<p>If your integers have certains properties like being INT32 and/or always positive you can use tricks like SWAR that will leverage SIMD instructions.</p>
<p><a href="https://lemire.me/blog/2022/01/21/swar-explained-parsing-eight-digits/" rel="nofollow noreferrer">https://lemire.me/blog/2022/01/21/swar-explained-parsing-eight-digits/</a></p>
<p>You can use AVX instructions if available to subtract ‘0’ on multiple bytes and detect \n on a range of bytes. This will be usually faster than more ‘normal’ algorithms.</p>
<p>For example to subtract ‘0’, if available you can do (with AVX2):</p>
<pre class="lang-cpp prettyprint-override"><code>const __m256i ascii_zero = _mm256_set1_epi8(0x30);

__m256i input = _mm256_load_si256((const __m256i*)(start + offset));

input = _mm256_sub_epi8(input, ascii_zero);  
</code></pre>
